This is a Behavioral pattern that you actually use every single time you write Java, but today you are going to
understand exactly how the engine works underneath.
The Problem: Exposing Your Internal Guts

Imagine you are building a Library application. You store your Book objects in a standard Java Array (Book[]).
The Junior Developer wants to print all the books on the UI, so they write this code in the WebController:
Java

// The Web Controller has to know exactly how the Library stores data!
Library library = new Library();
Book[] allBooks = library.getBooksArray();

for (int i = 0; i < allBooks.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(allBooks[i].getTitle());
}

The Architectural Danger:
What happens if, six months later, the Senior Developer realizes an Array is too slow, and changes the Library to use a
LinkedList, a TreeSet, or a HashMap?
Because the UI used [i] and .length, the UI completely breaks. The Web Controller is tightly coupled to the specific
data structure of the database!

The Senior Solution: The Iterator
The Iterator Pattern says: "Never let the outside world know how you store your data."

Instead of handing the UI an Array or a List, the Library hands the UI a tiny robotic object called an Iterator.
The Iterator only has two buttons:

   1.hasNext(): "Are there any books left?"
   2.next(): "Give me the next book."

The UI presses those two buttons. It has absolutely no idea if it is traversing an Array, a Tree, or a Graph. If the database changes its internal storage tomorrow, the UI code does not change by a single line.